\new@label@record{__tag_graphic.16}{{xpos}{4403946}{ypos}{21890780}{abspage}{38}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {15}{\ignorespaces Normalized influence weight of one probed agent. Source relation: original project variational-server diagnostic; estimand: normalized influence weight; uncertainty: deterministic seeded sweep. The weight is shown as a function of the agent's drift toward a confident-wrong belief (delta distribution on the wrong state), under \breaktt {variational\_aggregate} versus the naive log-linear pool. x-axis: outlier drift --- the mixing parameter carrying the probed agent's belief from the consensus posterior (zero, at consensus) to the confidently-wrong delta (one, full delta), increasing left to right; y-axis: normalized influence weight of the probed agent in the server weight vector. Under \breaktt {variational\_aggregate} (falling curve) the weight collapses below \(0.001\) as the agent goes extreme, while the naive pool holds it fixed at \(1/n = 0.143\) regardless (flat line). This demonstrates redescending normalized-weight behavior on the tested path; the algebraic theorem bounds the raw effective weight, but the figure is not an estimator-level B-robustness proof. Deterministic seeded sweep over \(n = 7\) agents; no error band, and no claim that the sharper \breaktt {robust\_aggregate} heuristic inherits this bound.}}{37}{figure.caption.79}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:bounded-influence}{{15}{37}{Normalized influence weight of one probed agent. Source relation: original project variational-server diagnostic; estimand: normalized influence weight; uncertainty: deterministic seeded sweep. The weight is shown as a function of the agent's drift toward a confident-wrong belief (delta distribution on the wrong state), under \protect \breaktt {variational\_aggregate} versus the naive log-linear pool. x-axis: outlier drift --- the mixing parameter carrying the probed agent's belief from the consensus posterior (zero, at consensus) to the confidently-wrong delta (one, full delta), increasing left to right; y-axis: normalized influence weight of the probed agent in the server weight vector. Under \protect \breaktt {variational\_aggregate} (falling curve) the weight collapses below \(0.001\) as the agent goes extreme, while the naive pool holds it fixed at \(1/n = 0.143\) regardless (flat line). This demonstrates redescending normalized-weight behavior on the tested path; the algebraic theorem bounds the raw effective weight, but the figure is not an estimator-level B-robustness proof. Deterministic seeded sweep over \(n = 7\) agents; no error band, and no claim that the sharper \protect \breaktt {robust\_aggregate} heuristic inherits this bound}{figure.caption.79}{}}
\citation{mildner2025fedgvi}
\new@label@record{__tag_graphic.17}{{xpos}{4404391}{ypos}{20414891}{abspage}{39}}
\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {16}{\ignorespaces Held-out classification accuracy of the federated Bayesian baseline. Source relation: original project FedGVI complement; estimand: clean held-out accuracy fraction; uncertainty: seed-level bootstrap interval. The \emph  {logistic-regression} baseline (5 clients, 200 points per class per client, gradient-descent point-estimate weights --- no posterior covariance is computed for this anchor) as a function of per-client label-contamination fraction. x-axis: contamination fraction (fraction of each client's labels flipped); y-axis: held-out classification accuracy on a clean test set, averaged over 64 independent seeds. The standard configuration (\texttt {nll} loss / \texttt {KLD} regularizer) and the robust FedGVI configuration (\texttt {rcce} loss / \texttt {AR} regularizer, \(q=1.00\)) are shown as separate curves; shaded bands show seed-level 95\% bootstrap intervals. The two curves are close at low-to-moderate contamination, separate over the moderate-to-high range (peak margin at 0.35 contamination), then reconverge at the highest swept level, where both decline sharply and neither curve reliably leads --- that level is included rather than omitted, since it is the one part of the sweep that does not favor the robust client. Note: this figure plots the NumPy logistic-regression anchor, \textbf  {not} the separate PyTorch deterministic MLP of the final paragraph (whose 16-hidden-unit, \(\mitbeta =0.5\) configuration is an executed point-mass-family complement). The recovery identities establish compatibility at the named limit; the per-client bounded-influence result belongs to the cited FedGVI theorem under its matching assumptions, distinct from the server-side heuristic reweighting shown in the robustness results. Each point and interval is computed across 64 independent seeds.}}{38}{figure.caption.80}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:bnn-robustness}{{16}{38}{Held-out classification accuracy of the federated Bayesian baseline. Source relation: original project FedGVI complement; estimand: clean held-out accuracy fraction; uncertainty: seed-level bootstrap interval. The \emph {logistic-regression} baseline (5 clients, 200 points per class per client, gradient-descent point-estimate weights --- no posterior covariance is computed for this anchor) as a function of per-client label-contamination fraction. x-axis: contamination fraction (fraction of each client's labels flipped); y-axis: held-out classification accuracy on a clean test set, averaged over 64 independent seeds. The standard configuration (\protect \texttt {nll} loss / \protect \texttt {KLD} regularizer) and the robust FedGVI configuration (\protect \texttt {rcce} loss / \protect \texttt {AR} regularizer, \(q=1.00\)) are shown as separate curves; shaded bands show seed-level 95\% bootstrap intervals. The two curves are close at low-to-moderate contamination, separate over the moderate-to-high range (peak margin at 0.35 contamination), then reconverge at the highest swept level, where both decline sharply and neither curve reliably leads --- that level is included rather than omitted, since it is the one part of the sweep that does not favor the robust client. Note: this figure plots the NumPy logistic-regression anchor, \textbf {not} the separate PyTorch deterministic MLP of the final paragraph (whose 16-hidden-unit, \(\beta =0.5\) configuration is an executed point-mass-family complement). The recovery identities establish compatibility at the named limit; the per-client bounded-influence result belongs to the cited FedGVI theorem under its matching assumptions, distinct from the server-side heuristic reweighting shown in the robustness results. Each point and interval is computed across 64 independent seeds}{figure.caption.80}{}}
